WEBVTT

00:08:58.956 --> 00:09:01.836
It's been suggestion
that he uses that play,

00:09:01.836 --> 00:09:04.236
a quaint - queynte - cunt,

00:09:04.276 --> 00:09:07.036
in his Sonnet number 20.

00:09:07.036 --> 00:09:08.316
Here he is.

00:09:08.316 --> 00:09:11.276
It certainly turns up
in a lot of his work.

00:09:11.276 --> 00:09:13.996
It's a lot ruder than we often
give him credit for.

00:09:13.996 --> 00:09:15.156
In Hamlet,

00:09:15.196 --> 00:09:18.356
act three, scene two,
Hamlet says to Ophelia, he says,

00:09:18.396 --> 00:09:19.636
"Shall I lie in your lap?"

00:09:19.676 --> 00:09:21.236
And she says, "Oh, no, my Lord."

00:09:21.236 --> 00:09:23.916
And then he says, "Do you think
I meant country matters?"

00:09:23.956 --> 00:09:26.116
When David Tennant
played that part, he paused

00:09:26.156 --> 00:09:28.716
"Do you think I meant
count-ry matters?"

00:09:28.716 --> 00:09:30.876
to try and really drive it home.